          { name: "Demographic Diversity & Specific Community Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "No single demographic group comprises a majority in HD-6: white 52.66%, Black 22.78%, Hispanic 17.89%, Asian 1.09%, with ~5.6% AIAN and multiracial. This explicit diversity means that demonstrating active, visible, and specific engagement with each community — rather than treating the district as a generic majority-minority coalition — is both a governance obligation and an electoral necessity. The 100% unopposed margin provides no evidence that the incumbent has built durable relationships across all five communities simultaneously, creating a latent vulnerability if a challenger invests in visible, differentiated constituent attention to each group's specific needs and concerns." },
          { name: "The Strategic Weakness of an Unopposed Majority", tag: "careful", why: "A 100% result reflects the absence of opposition, not demonstrated constituent dominance — and in a young (33.7), renter-heavy (39.5%), economically constrained ($50,273) district, this is not a floor but a potential liability if a credible challenger emerges with a coherent economic message and visible relationships across multiple communities. The unopposed margin provides no data on whether the incumbent has actually mobilized young renters, Black constituents, Hispanic constituents, or Native American residents around specific policy wins or whether these communities default to the incumbent by inertia. A future challenger who recognizes this gap and invests in genuine constituent engagement can quickly erode what appears to be a secure margin." },
          { name: "Treating Diversity as a Single Coalition Category", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where no demographic group exceeds 53% and the constituency includes distinct Black, Hispanic, white working-class, Asian, and Native American communities, aggregating diverse constituents into a generic 'coalition' rather than engaging each community's specific economic and social needs is governance failure. Housing affordability means something different for a young Hispanic family and a young Black single parent and a white working-class renter household — and the incumbent who treats these as a single constituency rather than five distinct communities will find that each group feels its specific concerns are invisible to its representative." },
          { name: "Assuming an Unopposed Majority Is Secure Against Future Opposition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% result provides no data on durability — it only reflects that no one ran a credible alternative in 2024. In a district this young, this renter-heavy, and this economically constrained, the absence of demonstrated incumbent investment in specific constituency relationships across all five demographic communities represents a clear opening for a future challenger. Any candidate or incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 100% represents secure consolidation rather than unopposed default is strategically misreading the district's actual vulnerability to organized opposition built around economic security and diverse community engagement." }
