          "The district's demographic composition — 71.35% white, 14.15% Black, 5.92% Hispanic, 3.69% Asian, with roughly 5% unaccounted for in the four reported categories — signals an urban community with meaningful racial diversity but without a single dominant minority population. This heterogeneous composition requires constituent attention that is genuinely distributed across these communities rather than defaulting to a white working-class or white college-educated electoral narrative. The 40% college-education rate and $71,063 income suggest a community of mixed educational and economic profiles: some households with professional employment and college degrees, others with high-school education and service-sector or skilled-trade work. A representative who treats this as a single 'urban' constituency without attention to these internal differences will fail to service the material concerns of renters experiencing housing-cost pressure, service workers navigating healthcare access, and working families managing childcare and education costs alongside their property taxes and wages."
