          { name: "Homeowner Property Tax & Cost-of-Living Pressure", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 75% homeowner majority at $75,638 median income — modest for suburban Indianapolis — faces real pressure on property taxes, insurance costs, and overall housing affordability. Republican messaging on property tax fairness and government spending efficiency has traction with this constituency, particularly if inflation erodes household purchasing power faster than wages grow. A Democratic incumbent who fails to engage homeowner cost-of-living concerns directly invites a Republican challenger to consolidate this base and pull swing homeowners away. Moseley must address property tax management and cost containment as primary concerns for the homeowner supermajority, not secondary issues." },
          { name: "Mixed-Education Swing Voter Persuasion", tag: "careful", why: "At 20.5% college attainment, this is not a college-educated professional district where educational credentials function as a reliable partisan signal; instead, the 79.5% non-degreed majority includes business owners, tradespeople, service-sector workers, and other professionals whose voting patterns follow economic outcomes and local governance competence rather than educational identity. This swing constituency can move in either direction depending on whether they perceive government as effective, costs as manageable, and their economic prospects as stable. A Democratic incumbent must communicate competence and economic benefit concretely; a Republican challenger who emphasizes government efficiency and business-friendly policy can credibly compete for these voters. Neither candidate can assume education level predicts partisan loyalty." },
          { name: "Treating 52% as a Durable Democratic Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52-48 result is a plurality, not a majority, in a district with a 25% renter rate, low college attainment, and a homeowner constituency facing cost-of-living pressure. If Moseley governs this seat as if 52% represents a secure Democratic base or assumes that 2024's national suburban shift toward Democrats is permanent, a competent Republican challenger who focuses on property tax management and inflation relief can easily construct a winning coalition. The 48% opposition is not a defeated minority — it is a substantial constituency whose material concerns remain unresolved and whose participation in the next cycle is uncertain." },
          { name: "Ignoring the ~3.24% Demographic Gap", tag: "avoid", why: "While the ~3.24% gap in the four Census categories is modest relative to other districts in this sequence, it likely represents American Indian residents, multiracial households, and other constituencies whose specific needs and community composition deserve recognition. Treating this population as negligible invites a challenger to build community relationships and demonstrate respect for constituencies that have been demographically invisible. Even a small shift in how this population participates can affect a 52-48 margin." }
