          "The district's racial and ethnic composition (67.21% White, 16.96% Asian, 7.24% Hispanic/Latino, 4.88% Black, with ~3.7% other) shows meaningful diversity without a single non-White group commanding majority share. This distribution is politically significant: it means cultural inclusion and anti-discrimination messaging will resonate with roughly one-quarter of the electorate as a direct identity issue, while the college-educated majority (across all groups) tends to view diversity and pluralism as a civic norm. Candidates who engage authentically with Asian and Latino residents on their specific economic and social concerns—and who signal respect for immigrant and multicultural identity—will hold the coalition. Those who deploy dog-whistle or exclusionary framing will fracture it."
