          "This district is fundamentally shaped by economic precarity. A median household income of $44,916 combined with a 46.3% renter rate creates a constituency where housing cost burden is likely the primary household financial stressor. The Democratic incumbent's 55% margin suggests voters are open to progressive governance but have not granted a mandate; the 45% Republican floor indicates consistent, competitive Republican support. This is a district of persuadable working and lower-middle-class voters, not a safely blue constituency, and it will remain competitive if Democratic messaging loses focus on material conditions. The relatively low college-education rate (25.4%) means candidates must communicate in concrete, jargon-free terms and avoid elite-coded language that signals distance from working-class life.",
