          "Investigate and address the 27% opposition directly: understand specifically whether it is concentrated among renters, younger adults, or the Hispanic community, and whether it reflects economic anxiety, specific governance failures, or insufficient engagement with particular constituencies. In a district this favorable to Republicans, opposition of this size is a signal of something concrete to fix, not a demographic inevitability to ignore.",
          "Engage the Hispanic community (12.4%) with active constituent service and specific attention to the economic concerns of agricultural and service-sector workers: this population is not reliably Republican in presidential elections and can be a swing constituency in a competitive cycle if the incumbent neglects genuine engagement.",
          "Avoid the trap of treating 73% as a safe permanent majority: it is a clear win but not a dominant mandate, and any challenger who consolidates the 27% opposition while winning 5-7 points of persuadable voters from the incumbent's coalition will flip the seat. Continuous engagement with working-class economic concerns is the foundation of seat durability, not partisan registration patterns."
