          "Engage the 20.4% Hispanic community with visible, ongoing constituent service and specific policy attention; unopposed status creates an opportunity to build that relationship now rather than wait for a challenger to mobilize it. Workplace protections, small-business support, and education pathways for working families are issues where Hispanic constituents' material concerns align with the broader working-class economic agenda.",
          "Address the college-attainment gap directly: at 23.4% college-educated, HD-49 has the second-lowest rate in the comparison sequence, meaning most constituents follow non-traditional workforce pathways. Vocational training funding, apprenticeship promotion, and employer partnerships that create quality work without four-year degrees are concrete governance priorities for a district whose educational reality does not match assumptions about college as the default pathway.",
          "Avoid complacency around the unopposed 2024 result: a 100% margin reflects the absence of a challenger, not universal approval of the incumbent's record. Any future opposition that runs on working-class economic security, Hispanic community engagement, and housing affordability could rapidly close the gap if the current representative has not built specific constituent relationships and a visible record of delivery."
