          { name: "Renter Population Service Access & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 30.1% renters—a meaningful minority in a homeowner-dominant district—this population has distinct concerns around housing costs, tenant protections, and service delivery in rural areas where rental housing stock may be limited and deteriorating. Unopposed incumbency often leads to overlooking non-homeowner constituencies; active engagement with renter needs around affordability and service access prevents a challenger from mobilizing this population as a swing constituency." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap & Potential Indigenous or Minority Constituent Needs", tag: "careful", why: "The five reported demographic categories account for only 94.7% of the district, leaving a 5.3% gap likely representing American Indian, Alaska Native, or other resident populations. In rural Indiana districts, this gap may be small enough to overlook strategically, but systematic inattention to any population not visible in standard reporting creates vulnerability to a challenger who specifically mobilizes or serves those constituents. The gap deserves research and authentic engagement, not assumption of invisibility." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Incumbency as Permission for Constituent Neglect", tag: "avoid", why: "A 2024 unopposed result is not confirmation of a safe seat; it is absence of opposition, which may reflect weakness of the challenger environment rather than strength of the incumbent's constituent relationships. Complacency during unopposed cycles—failing to maintain granular understanding of district economic concerns, service gaps, or demographic diversity—creates vulnerability to a well-resourced challenger in the next competitive cycle. The 5.3% demographic gap and 30.1% renter population are particularly at risk of becoming invisible during unopposed incumbency." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 20.1% College-Attainment Rate as a Long-Term Workforce Development Signal", tag: "avoid", why: "With only 20.1% college-educated—among the lowest rates in comparable Midwestern districts—IN-HD-56 is not generating local workforce opportunity or educational attainment growth at the rate needed to retain younger adults or attract new investment. Ignoring this as a long-term structural concern means younger adults perceive limited economic future locally and become persuadable by a challenger offering education, workforce development, or economic diversification initiatives. This is a slow-moving but real vulnerability in a district with an aging population and modest income." }
