          { name: "Property Tax Fairness & Fiscal Management", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $91,605 median household income with 76.7% homeownership, property taxes are the largest direct interaction most constituents have with local government and state policy. In a district where household net worth is heavily concentrated in home equity, tax fairness, predictability, and the quality of services funded by those taxes are not abstract fiscal questions but direct household financial concerns. Any candidate who can demonstrate fiscal competence and stewardship of taxpayer resources while delivering visible, well-maintained public services is speaking directly to what this constituency evaluates on. Conversely, any incumbent perceived as indifferent to property tax burden or wasteful public spending is vulnerable to a challenger who makes this the centerpiece of a campaign." },
          { name: "Education Quality & School System Performance", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 32.5% college-educated adults and a 40.3-year median age, this is a constituency with above-average education attainment and a significant proportion with school-age or recently-school-age children. Education quality is both an immediate household concern (for those with kids in district schools) and a long-term property-value concern (good schools drive home prices). A legislator who can point to demonstrable improvements in school funding, teacher quality, educational outcomes, and curriculum alignment with both college preparation and workforce development is building the constituent relationship that holds a seat like this. A legislator perceived as indifferent to school quality or reluctant to fund education adequately is vulnerable to a challenger who makes this a centerpiece." },
          { name: "Demographic Stability & Small Unaccounted-For Population", tag: "careful", why: "The approximately 3% gap in the demographic data is small relative to the district's 85.36% white majority, but in a district this affluent and stable, even small populations deserve specific constituent attention rather than invisibility. This gap likely includes Native American residents, and possibly some Asian or other minority households not fully captured in the reported categories. While not large enough to swing election outcomes, ignoring these constituencies entirely is both a governance failure and a missed relationship-building opportunity with voters who may appreciate explicit acknowledgment of their presence and needs." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Durable Incumbency", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 election is an artifact of the opposition party's candidate recruitment failure or strategic choice, not evidence of Haggard's strength or electoral durability. In a district with above-median income, above-median education, and an older, homeowner-dominant constituency, voters have the economic security and cognitive resources to evaluate candidates on governance performance rather than inherited partisan loyalty. A credible challenger who enters the field and makes a strong case on fiscal management, education quality, or constituent service responsiveness could find this seat far more competitive than the unopposed 2024 result suggests." },
          { name: "Assuming Abstract Conservative Positioning Substitutes for Demonstrated Results", tag: "avoid", why: "At 32.5% college attainment and $91,605 income, HD-57 is an educated, affluent constituency that evaluates policy on evidence and demonstrated outcomes, not ideological positioning. A legislator who relies on abstract conservative rhetoric while delivering ambiguous or absent results on the fiscal management, education quality, and infrastructure maintenance that this district actually cares about is vulnerable to a challenger — including potentially a moderate Republican or independent — who can point to concrete accomplishments. This is not a district where partisan identity is sufficient to hold the seat; governance competence is the necessary condition." }
