          { name: "College-Educated Voter Engagement on Governance Competence", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 27.2% college attainment, HD-60's educated constituency evaluates representatives on specific governance outcomes — school quality, efficient local administration, infrastructure performance — rather than partisan messaging or ideological alignment alone. This is a group with the information access and critical capacity to hold elected officials accountable to measurable results rather than promises. Building credibility through documented local accomplishment and transparent performance metrics is more persuasive with this constituency than rhetoric or party affiliation, and provides both a stronger hold on existing support and a genuine vulnerability if an incumbent's record is perceived as underperforming." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap and Emerging Community Composition", tag: "careful", why: "The 3.34% demographic gap in a district where the reported categories sum to only 96.66% is small in absolute terms but potentially significant in strategic terms. In a prosperous, majority-white district, this gap may represent immigrant families, younger professionals, or other newer residents whose political preferences and turnout patterns are less predictable than the settled homeowner majority. The 76-24 result does not tell us whether these residents voted 76-24 or whether they simply did not appear in standard turnout samples. Understanding who comprises this gap and what their actual voting behavior is could reveal either an emerging demographic shift requiring preemptive engagement or a stable group already aligned with the incumbent — either way, assuming they are politically inert is strategically dangerous." },
          { name: "Treating 76% as a Durable Supermajority Rather Than a Specific Result Requiring Explanation", tag: "avoid", why: "A district with this demographic profile — prosperous, highly homeowning, college-educated, stable median age — should produce Republican margins in the high 70s or low 80s as a baseline. The fact that Mayfield achieved 76%, not 82%, suggests either modest ticket-splitting or specific local factors that prevented a more dominant outcome. Any incumbent who assumes that 76% is a floor rather than asking what prevented 82% is failing to understand the actual electoral dynamics of their district." },
          { name: "Complacency About Demographic Stability Masking Vulnerability to Credible Opposition", tag: "avoid", why: "The apparent stability of HD-60 — settled homeowners, high income, college-educated, low renter rate — can create false confidence that the seat is electorally safe without active constituent engagement. A challenger with credible local standing, specific appeal to the 3.34% demographic gap, or a message tailored to younger adults' concerns (healthcare costs, education affordability, economic opportunity) could find substantially more traction than the 76% headline suggests. Mayfield's vulnerability increases directly with any assumption that the seat will hold itself without active work to consolidate homeowner support and build relationship with emerging communities." }
