          "The 69.7% renter rate is the most actionable strategic lever. In a district where nearly 7 in 10 households do not own property, housing policy is not an abstraction—it is direct leverage over disposable income, neighborhood stability, and life planning. Renters at $44,201 median income are acutely vulnerable to rent increases, eviction, and displacement. Messaging must connect housing policy to downstream outcomes: student success (reduce housing instability), workforce retention (make skilled jobs viable by stabilizing housing costs), and long-term wealth building (policies that create pathways to ownership). The demographic composition is 75.32% White and 4.83–10.14% each for other groups, indicating limited racial polarization as a primary electoral driver; instead, class and age are the operative cleavages, and housing is the fulcrum."
