          { name: "Suburban Homeowner Economic Security & Property-Tax Fairness", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 80.4% homeownership and $74,758 median income, HD-66 is an older suburban community where property values and property-tax burden are the primary household financial concerns. The 39.3-year median age suggests a constituency approaching peak property-tax sensitivity—families with children in school systems, equity in homes, and fixed incomes increasingly affected by rising property tax assessments. Any incumbent who neglects property-tax fairness, school funding adequacy relative to tax burden, or infrastructure investment that protects neighborhood stability will find homeowner turnout suppressed by frustration, even in a 69% Republican district. This is the foundational issue for margin consolidation." },
          { name: "K-12 Education Quality & College-Educated Professional Satisfaction", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 23.5% college-educated population in a high-income suburb is not passively Republican—it evaluates on education quality, teacher compensation, and whether the school system is preparing students for competitive postsecondary opportunity. Indiana's education funding challenges and teacher compensation gaps relative to neighboring states are salient grievances for college-educated professionals, particularly when Democrats offer specific education investment proposals. Leading with concrete achievement data, teacher recruitment/retention results, and education quality metrics—rather than abstract fiscal conservatism—is essential to holding this segment's consolidation within the Republican coalition." },
          { name: "Demographic Drift & Emerging Multiracial and Immigrant Communities", tag: "careful", why: "The ~3.85% demographic gap and relatively low Hispanic (3.75%) and Asian (0.79%) percentages mask potential demographic growth in Indiana's suburbs—a structural trend in metropolitan counties that can accumulate quickly over one or two redistricting cycles. While currently small enough to be electorally negligible, any candidate who treats emerging communities with indifference or exclusion from economic development and constituent service narratives is ceding future organizing opportunity to Democratic challengers. Proactive, genuine inclusion—not performative outreach—is the investment that prevents this segment from becoming mobilized opposition as it grows." },
          { name: "Treating 69% as a Durable Structural Majority Rather Than an Active Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "The 69% result is superficially dominant but is built on three specific constituencies—homeowners, college-educated professionals, and consolidated Anglo voters—each of which can fracture under specific electoral conditions. An incumbent who campaigns this seat as though the 69% is insurmountable and neglects homeowner economic grievances, education quality concerns, or professional-class satisfaction is arithmetically wrong about the structural durability of the margin. Any Democratic challenger who can simultaneously depress suburban homeowner turnout while mobilizing college-educated professionals on education and healthcare has a credible path to competitive viability in this district." },
          { name: "Assuming the 31% Democratic Baseline Is Inert Rather Than Potentially Mobilizable", tag: "avoid", why: "The 31% Democratic result in a high-income, college-educated suburb is not a ceiling—it is a floor that contains young professionals, college-educated women, minority-community voters, and suburban professionals frustrated with current governance. A 10-12 point swing in mobilization or candidate quality between a complacent Republican campaign and an energized Democratic challenger could move this district from 69-31 to 60-40 or closer. Any incumbent who assumes the opposition base is inherently unmotivated or electorally incompetent is overestimating the durability of a 38-point margin that depends entirely on active Republican consolidation and Democratic disengagement." }
