          { name: "Wage Growth and Workforce Development Pathways", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 39.5 median age and 22.7% college attainment suggest a working-class younger-adult constituency that evaluates candidates on economic mobility and actual wage growth, not educational access rhetoric. At $62,976 income, this district's households are stretched; wage policy, apprenticeship programs, and sector-specific training that lead to tangible income improvement are more resonant than traditional education messaging. Chesser's ability to articulate and advance a wage-growth legislative agenda is critical to consolidating her younger-adult base." },
          { name: "Native American and Alaska Native Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The approximately 5.79% gap between reported Census categories and 100% likely represents American Indian and Alaska Native residents whose political preferences and turnout patterns are not yet established in the current partisan data. In a 52-48 race, even a modest shift in AIAN voter preference or turnout would materially affect the outcome. Early, genuine engagement with whatever tribal communities or AIAN populations exist in HD-71 prevents the opposition from building credibility with these voters by default." },
          { name: "Campaigning the 52% Result as Though It Represents a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52-48 result is a plurality in a close race, not a mandate or a floor. In a working-class, renter-heavy district without strong partisan institutional infrastructure, a 48% opposition is actively evaluating alternatives; Chesser's reelection is contingent on demonstrating legislative delivery on the material economic concerns that drove her 52%, not on assuming that margin will persist absent new information about her effectiveness." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Education and Credentialism Messaging in a Working-Class District", tag: "avoid", why: "At 22.7% college attainment — below the statewide average — and a 39.5 median age skewed toward working-class adults navigating immediate economic pressure, abstract messaging about higher education and credential attainment misses what this constituency actually needs. Wage growth, cost-of-living relief, and economic mobility without requiring a four-year degree are more resonant than education policy rhetoric; defaulting to education framing signals that Chesser does not fully understand HD-71's economic reality." }
