          { name: "Renter Economic Security & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 45.6% renter rate with $49,058 median income, HD-80 has a constituency where housing costs consume a disproportionate share of household resources. Rent stabilization, tenant protections, eviction prevention, and local housing development that targets affordable units are not aspirational policy — they are survival economics for nearly half the district. A representative who addresses housing affordability with concrete legislative action demonstrates material constituent service that resonates across the district's plural demographic base." },
          { name: "Educational Attainment & Workforce Mobility Pipeline", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 14.9% college attainment rate is the lowest in a ten-district sequence and represents an 85% population without four-year credentials competing in an economy that increasingly demands post-secondary skills. Community college affordability, trade apprenticeships, employer-sponsored credential programs, and wage-ladder employment are the practical mechanisms for improving household income and economic security in a district where educational mobility is the primary constraint on upward movement. A legislative agenda that builds pathways rather than relying on traditional bachelor's degrees reflects the actual economic reality of HD-80's constituency." },
          { name: "Demographic Plurality & Differentiated Constituency Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "With no single demographic group commanding a majority (37% white, 27% Black, 19% Hispanic, 11% Asian, ~6% AIAN/other), HD-80 requires differentiated engagement across multiple constituencies rather than consolidated coalition-building. Black constituents' concerns around healthcare access and workforce representation differ from Hispanic constituents' concerns around immigration policy clarity and economic mobility; Asian constituents' concerns around educational access and professional advancement differ from white working-class constituents' concerns around housing stability. Treating these constituencies as an undifferentiated 'coalition' rather than addressing specific, constituency-particular concerns invites a challenger to offer more targeted engagement." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Seat Security", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% unopposed result tells you that no credible opponent emerged in 2024 — not that the incumbent has built unshakeable relationships with a district facing genuine economic stress. In a constituency with 45.6% renters, 14.9% college attainment, and only $49,058 income, the absence of opposition likely reflects political fragmentation or low engagement rather than incumbent strength. Any candidate or analyst who assumes this seat is demographically locked-in and safely Democratic is ignoring the material economic precarity that creates openings for opponents willing to address those concerns directly." },
          { name: "Assuming Demographic Trend Substitutes for Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-80's demographic composition (majority-minority, young-median-age at 32.4, high renting, low education) trends Democratic in national electoral patterns, but demographic trend is not constituent service. If the incumbent treats the seat as held by demographic inevitability rather than by delivering concrete improvements to housing security, educational opportunity, and economic mobility, a challenger who offers material economic policy will find a receptive constituency. In working-class districts, demographic alignment without economic outcomes is insufficient to hold an unopposed seat indefinitely once competition emerges." }
