          "Demographics reveal a White-dominant district (65.4%) with meaningful Asian representation (12.49%)—the second-largest group and growing in relevance—plus small but present Black (9.03%) and Hispanic/Latino (8.25%) populations. The slight gap to 100% likely reflects American Indian/Alaska Native residents. This composition argues against polarizing ethnic or immigrant-focused rhetoric, which risks alienating the Asian community and unnecessarily energizing the working-class White voters already leaning Republican. Instead, winning candidates will build coalitions around shared economic interest: renter protections, wage floors, job training, and small-business support that cut across lines. The youth profile (median age 35.1) suggests a constituency still forming long-term attachments; early economic wins can lock in durable support."
