          "The 81% result indicates the electorate has rejected the opposition wholesale and is not persuadable by traditional swing messaging. Competitive threats emerge only from Democratic primary challengers, low engagement, or loss of trust due to perceived misalignment with bread-and-butter economic concerns. A candidate—incumbent or challenger—wins and holds this seat by becoming synonymous with rent affordability, wage growth, job training, and responsive constituent service. The renter majority and college-education gap mean abstract economic arguments matter far less than specific, local policy wins: a rent stabilization ordinance, a funded apprenticeship pipeline, responsive 311-style constituent service, or visible neighborhood investment. Messaging should be grounded in the tangible experience of the 56% renting at the $50K income level, not in partisan abstractions."
