          "The strategic vulnerability in IN-SD-5 is not immediate—a 32-point margin is durable—but embedded in the district's demographic composition: college-educated professionals and affluent homeowners have become less reliably Republican in statewide and national politics since 2016, particularly on issues of healthcare access, education funding, and governance competence. A $81,594 median income and 28.4% college attainment signal a constituency of white-collar professionals, small business owners, and dual-income households whose economic interests (tax stability, quality schools, property values, healthcare access) remain generally Republican-aligned, but whose cultural and social orientation increasingly diverges from partisan extremism on education and civic participation. The 41.4 median age—neither particularly young nor particularly old—indicates a constituency in active working and child-rearing years, making schools, property taxes, and family healthcare access directly material concerns. Charbonneau's 66% result holds this coalition, but it is not a coalition that can be taken for granted; any challenger who can credibly compete for the college-educated professional vote on governance quality, education investment, and moderate Republican positioning will find this district substantially more competitive than the current margin suggests."
