          { name: "Workforce Development & Skills Training", tag: "lean-into", why: "Only 24.2% of adults are college-educated—well below national benchmarks—while median household income sits at $69,435. This signals a district reliant on skilled trades, manufacturing, or non-degree credentials. Messaging on vocational pathways, apprenticeships, and local job quality directly addresses the economic reality of the plurality who do not hold degrees." },
          { name: "Immigration & Demographic Change", tag: "careful", why: "Hispanic/Latino residents comprise 8.71% of the district—a meaningful but minority share in a district that is 83.1% white. This population is large enough to notice and engage, but messaging must be calibrated carefully; heavy emphasis on demographic change itself may alienate the white majority without persuading Latino voters on substantive grounds. Focus messaging on economic opportunity and community integration rather than demographic framing." },
          { name: "Cultural Identity & Values Signaling", tag: "avoid", why: "The unopposed 2024 result (100% Republican, 0% Democratic) reflects not just strength but the absence of competitive pressure to test messaging. Assuming an incumbent or Republican candidate, culture-war framing risks overplaying a hand in a district where bread-and-butter economics (median income $69,435, renter rate 29.3%) are the lived baseline. Avoid gratuitous polarization that does not address material voter concerns." },
          { name: "Higher Education & Credentialism", tag: "avoid", why: "With 75.8% of adults lacking a bachelor's degree, messaging that privileges college attendance or assumes college-educated professionals as the primary audience will misalign with district reality. Avoid rhetoric that implicitly devalues non-degree workers or frames college as the sole path to dignity and success; this district's workforce does not reflect that profile." }
