          { name: "Younger Family Economic Anxiety and Wage Stagnation", tag: "careful", why: "The 35.8-year median age and $62,297 income together signal a younger-to-middle-aged family constituency facing real economic pressure: stagnant wages, rising housing costs, and uncertainty about economic stability. These households are not defaulting to inherited partisan loyalty — they are evaluating whether government is delivering on their economic concerns. The unopposed incumbent's 2022 victory does not measure whether this constituency perceives the incumbent as responsive to their wage, housing, and family economic concerns. A challenger who speaks directly to younger family economic anxiety and offers concrete policy solutions can shift this constituency if the incumbent has not built authentic relationships around these material concerns." },
          { name: "Assuming Unopposed Status Indicates Political Strength", tag: "avoid", why: "Unopposed victories in state Senate races typically reflect temporary absence of opposition recruitment and resources, not necessarily deep constituent satisfaction or insurmountable incumbent strength. In a district with 37.3% renters and $62,297 median income, an unopposed incumbent may not have invested in understanding renter concerns, Hispanic community responsiveness, or younger families' economic anxieties. A well-resourced challenger who invests in these constituencies can quickly establish that the unopposed incumbent is out of touch with district needs." },
          { name: "Treating the 3.6% Demographic Gap as Negligible", tag: "avoid", why: "The unaccounted-for 3.6% of the population — likely Native American or multi-racial residents — may appear numerically small but often represents communities with specific governance concerns around representation, service access, and cultural recognition that, if neglected, can activate organizing and opposition. In Indiana Senate districts, these communities are often concentrated in specific neighborhoods or precincts, and their mobilization around specific issues (education, healthcare access, criminal justice) can shift precinct-level margins in a district this size. Treating this population as too small to warrant engagement is both a governance failure and an electoral vulnerability." }
