          { name: "Young Renter and Working-Family Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 28.1 median age and 39.8% renter rate with $58,331 household income, In-SD-23 is a young, working-class constituency for whom housing affordability, childcare access, and wage growth are direct quality-of-life pressures. A representative who demonstrates specific legislative accomplishments on these material concerns — not abstract positioning — builds the authentic constituent relationship that holds younger renters in a Republican coalition when they might otherwise shift. Messaging that speaks to the concrete financial pressures of renting families and working-age households is more electorally durable in this demographic than partisan appeals." },
          { name: "Manufacturing and Service-Sector Job Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 33.6% college attainment rate signals a constituency whose economic mobility depends on job availability and wage levels in sectors that do not require four-year degrees — manufacturing, skilled trades, healthcare support, and service work. A legislative agenda focused on workforce development, apprenticeship pathways, and support for employers who offer stable wages and benefits in these sectors directly addresses the career prospects of a constituency whose economic futures do not run through universities. This is where the district's younger workers are building their lives, and their voting behavior will reflect whether legislators take their employment prospects seriously." },
          { name: "Demographic Diversity and Community-Specific Needs", tag: "careful", why: "While In-SD-23 is 80.78% white, the 8.61% Asian, 4.96% Hispanic, and 2.67% Black populations are large enough to respond to targeted constituent service but can become invisible in campaigns that focus exclusively on the white majority. These communities have specific economic mobility concerns, healthcare access needs, and educational opportunities that warrant direct legislative attention rather than assumption that they will respond to generic minority outreach. A representative who builds genuine relationships with these communities can consolidate their support while avoiding the resentment that comes from visible constituency abandonment." },
          { name: "Treating 64% as a Durable Partisan Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 64-36 margin in a district with a 28.1 median age, 39.8% renter rate, and working-class income base is current alignment, not a structural Republican hold. Younger renters and working-age households without college degrees vote on economic outcomes more than partisan identity; if they conclude the incumbent is not addressing housing costs, childcare access, or wage stagnation, the 64% margin is vulnerable to erosion. A candidate who campaigns as though the seat is safe without demonstrating concrete economic accomplishments for working families is taking unnecessary electoral risk." },
          { name: "Abstract Conservative Positioning Over Kitchen-Table Economic Solutions", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency with 33.6% college attainment and $58,331 median income evaluates politicians primarily on whether they are delivering tangible economic relief, not on ideological consistency or partisan positioning. Generic conservative rhetoric about regulation, taxation, or government size is substantially less electorally relevant to a young renting family struggling with childcare costs than specific legislative proposals that address their household budget. A campaign strategy that relies on ideological messaging while ignoring the material economic concerns of the district's largest demographic cohorts is forfeiting the most reliable path to holding and expanding the current coalition." }
