          { name: "Local Governance and Service Delivery Competence", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 66% result in a district this affluent and educated, with an 8-10 point gap from demographic expectation, suggests the incumbent's margin rests partially on demonstrated competence rather than purely on partisan lean. Affluent, educated voters evaluate heavily on whether local government delivers reliable services, sound financial stewardship, and effective problem-solving — not on abstract ideological positioning. A candidate who can point to specific accomplishments in infrastructure, education funding, or public administration and demonstrate competent governance outperforms one who leads with partisan messaging in this constituency." },
          { name: "34% Opposition as Swing Signal Rather Than Peripheral", tag: "careful", why: "A 34% Democratic share in a district this affluent, educated, and demographically Republican is mathematically larger than typical affluent Republican districts and suggests that college-educated, professional voters are making deliberate choices to support the opposition on substantive grounds. Whether driven by reproductive rights positioning, education policy disagreement, climate or environmental concerns, or specific local governance dissatisfaction, this opposition is not noise — it is a signal of actual persuadable voters. In the next cycle, if the Republican incumbent or challenger does not address the specific concerns driving that 34%, the margin will narrow further toward competitive." },
          { name: "Treating 66% as a Durable Baseline", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% result paired with an 8-10 point demographic shortfall is not a durable baseline — it is a warning signal. The incumbent who assumes the margin is secure and campaigns toward consolidation of the existing 66% without addressing the concerns of the college-educated voters who are currently voting opposition will discover the margin narrowing in the next cycle. This district's educated, professional constituency is capable of shifting if they conclude their actual governance concerns are not being addressed." },
          { name: "Partisan Base-Building Without Addressing the 34%", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district of $94,632 median income and 36.7% college attainment, a campaign strategy that relies on energizing the Republican base while ignoring what is driving 34% of college-educated, professional voters to support the opposition is strategically incoherent. The 34% is not a trivial margin to surrender; it is a coalition of voters whose departure from Republican voting is deliberate and policy-driven. Ignoring this signal and assuming the base is enough to hold 66% misreads the arithmetic and the psychology of affluent, educated constituencies." }
