          { name: "Rural & Suburban Quality of Life", tag: "lean-into", why: "The median age of 40.7 and the demographic composition (82.85% white, with smaller minority populations) point to an established, family-oriented suburban and rural constituency prioritizing stability over change. This cohort responds to messaging around safe neighborhoods, good schools, infrastructure maintenance, and preserving community character. The 64% margin indicates deep alignment with a stability-oriented agenda; lean into themes of responsible stewardship and practical problem-solving that resonate with this demographic's life stage and priorities." },
          { name: "Education & Workforce Development", tag: "careful", why: "Only 20.4% of adults are college-educated, well below national average, meaning this district is composition-heavy in non-degree holders and trade/skilled workers. Education messaging must avoid implying that four-year degrees are the only path to dignified work or economic security. Instead, frame workforce development around vocational training, apprenticeships, and skilled trades that create immediate pathways to middle-class wages. Avoid condescension toward non-college workers or suggestions that higher education is a prerequisite for respect or success." },
          { name: "Racial Identity Politics & Demographic Change Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "The district is 82.85% white with Black (6.06%) and Hispanic (5.28%) populations each well below 10%. Explicit identity-based appeals or zero-sum framing of demographic change will activate Democratic turnout without moving meaningful Republican margins in a district already won decisively. Avoid language suggesting demographic groups compete for resources; instead, use inclusive civic framing that treats all residents as stakeholders in shared prosperity." },
          { name: "Cultural Grievance & Social Division Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "A 64-point margin indicates decisive victory without requiring culture-war intensity; doubling down on divisive social messaging risks alienating the moderate suburban and older-adult voters who comprise much of this base without creating new Republican converts. This district has already chosen its direction; sustain it through forward-looking governance messaging rather than grievance narratives that invite Democratic counter-mobilization." }
