          { name: "Healthcare Access & Cost Management", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district with 23.5% college attainment and $57,617 income, healthcare costs are a direct household budget concern, not a peripheral policy issue—particularly for a constituency with significant renter and early-career populations who may lack employer-sponsored coverage. Rural healthcare access, insurance affordability, and pharmaceutical cost management are the practical healthcare concerns for working-age families and older adults on fixed incomes. A legislative record that demonstrates concrete action on healthcare affordability—whether through insurance regulation, cost transparency, or provider access—directly addresses the material concerns of voters in SD-26." },
          { name: "Native American Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The ~5% AIAN gap in the demographic data represents approximately 2,500-3,000 people in the district whose civic and economic participation is present and real, even if modest in percentage terms. In Indiana's tribal territories, Native American voters have demonstrated organized civic participation around healthcare access, economic development, and tribal sovereignty concerns. While the AIAN population is too small to flip the seat on its own, authentic engagement—rather than invisible assumption—is both a governing obligation and a hedge against mobilized opposition." },
          { name: "Treating a 61-39 Margin as a Durable Foundation", tag: "avoid", why: "A 22-point victory is substantial, but it is built on a relatively modest income base ($57,617) and working-age population (37.0 median age) that evaluates on material economic outcomes rather than inherited partisan loyalty. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 61% represents a comfortable hold while ignoring the material concerns of working-class renters and early-career professionals creates an opening for a challenger who addresses those concerns directly. The margin is durable only if the incumbent is actively demonstrating delivery on the economic concerns that matter to the constituency." },
          { name: "Framing Housing & Renter Issues as Special Interests", tag: "avoid", why: "At 31.5% renters, housing affordability and tenant protections are not peripheral special-interest issues—they are core constituent concerns for roughly one-third of the district's households. Any legislator who frames renter protections, housing affordability, or tenant rights as identity-politics impositions on working-class homeowners will find that framing actively hostile to the material reality of one-third of the people they represent. Housing policy is an economic security issue for working-class renters, not an ideological battleground." }
