          "The 81.07% white, 6.93% Asian demographic composition, combined with high college attainment and youth, signals an educated urban or inner-ring suburban constituency. The 4.74% Hispanic and 3.7% Black populations, while smaller, represent constituencies whose economic and civic concerns can be rendered invisible in a district where the dominant story becomes educated white professionals. The renter rate of 46.9% is the most politically significant number in the district profile: renters at median age 30.4 and $62,789 income are acutely sensitive to housing costs, eviction protections, landlord regulation, and whether local government is investing in their economic stability. The current representative who won without opposition has no pressure to maintain authentic engagement with either the young renter majority or the smaller minority communities whose needs exist independent of broader partisan margins. This represents genuine vulnerability disguised as consensus—the kind of seat that flips when a challenger articulates what young renters actually need and demonstrates that current representation is absent."
